{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>A lion's whelp.</b> He likened him to a young lion's whelp, which is of the lion species when it grows; and there is in it no כף of comparison, like ועיר פרא (Job 11:12)." "<b>From the prey, my son, you have gone up.</b> You are like, my son, to a lion's whelp when you have gone up from the prey. And the meaning is מטרף אחר. And this is its plain sense. And if we interpret it concerning Joseph, it would have been fitting that in place of עלית it should be העלית, for עלית is a participle. And behold, the verse after it is לעד. And do you not see ותעל אחד מגוריה (Ezekiel 19:3) —" "And the meaning of כרע רבץ כאריה: the habit of the lion, after preying, is to sit on its haunches and lie down; and even if every species of beast passed by, it would not rise because of them to flee."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {1 "Reading עלית as a participle (פועל עומד) rather than perfect; Ibn Ezra argues a perfect form would require העלית if referring to Joseph's past ascent."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:48:25.490236770Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}